# Cove on a server of your own, at your domain, with every project at its own
# name: report.example.org, lab.example.org, ... (operator, 2026-11-22: "what
# does it take to get the subdomain to work generally").
#
# Caddy answers HTTPS for any name and hands every request to Cove, which
# knows its projects: a project's name goes to that project's port, private to
# whoever is signed in to this Cove unless the project is public; anything
# else is Cove itself (server.rs handle). A certificate is issued the first
# time a name is opened, and only after Cove says the name is one it serves
# (/__tls/ask): no certificate for names it does not serve, and no DNS keys
# on this server.
#
# Two things outside this server, once:
#   your DNS:   example.org and *.example.org -> this server's address
#   Cove:       running on 127.0.0.1:8788 (packaging/linux/cove@.service)
#
#   sudo cp Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && sudo systemctl reload caddy

{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:8788/__tls/ask
	}
}

https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8788 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
	}
}

http:// {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
